WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.976 align:middle line:90%
[SQUEAKING]

00:00:01.976 --> 00:00:03.952 align:middle line:90%
[RUSTLING]

00:00:03.952 --> 00:00:06.916 align:middle line:90%
[CLICKING]

00:00:06.916 --> 00:00:19.270 align:middle line:90%


00:00:19.270 --> 00:00:25.900 align:middle line:84%
So let's continue with
the big-name theorems,

00:00:25.900 --> 00:00:29.330 align:middle line:84%
or the theorems with big
names, or the big theorems

00:00:29.330 --> 00:00:38.210 align:middle line:84%
with names, which the last
one was a uniform boundedness

00:00:38.210 --> 00:00:44.480 align:middle line:84%
theorem, which followed from
Baire's category theorem, which

00:00:44.480 --> 00:00:46.400 align:middle line:90%
let me again recall for you.

00:00:46.400 --> 00:00:52.350 align:middle line:90%


00:00:52.350 --> 00:01:11.150 align:middle line:84%
If M is a complete
metric space, C k

00:01:11.150 --> 00:01:26.780 align:middle line:84%
is a collection of
closed sets and M

00:01:26.780 --> 00:01:41.650 align:middle line:84%
is equal to the union of these
closed sets, then at least one

00:01:41.650 --> 00:01:51.760 align:middle line:84%
of these closed sets
has an interior point.

00:01:51.760 --> 00:01:54.400 align:middle line:84%
Or another way of saying it,
at least one of these C k's

00:01:54.400 --> 00:01:59.510 align:middle line:90%
contains an open ball.

00:01:59.510 --> 00:02:03.220 align:middle line:90%


00:02:03.220 --> 00:02:06.820 align:middle line:84%
So now, what we're
going to prove

00:02:06.820 --> 00:02:09.699 align:middle line:84%
as a consequence of the
Baire category theorem

00:02:09.699 --> 00:02:20.570 align:middle line:84%
is so-called "open
mapping theorem," which

00:02:20.570 --> 00:02:24.890 align:middle line:84%
says that surjective linear,
bounded linear operators

00:02:24.890 --> 00:02:26.780 align:middle line:90%
send open sets to open sets.

00:02:26.780 --> 00:02:31.410 align:middle line:84%
It's kind of the backwards
version of continuity.

00:02:31.410 --> 00:02:48.520 align:middle line:84%
So if B1, B2 are
Banach spaces and T

00:02:48.520 --> 00:02:52.120 align:middle line:84%
is a bounded linear
operator from B1

00:02:52.120 --> 00:03:04.860 align:middle line:84%
to B2, which is
surjective, meaning onto,

00:03:04.860 --> 00:03:11.560 align:middle line:84%
then T is what's
called an "open map."

00:03:11.560 --> 00:03:13.640 align:middle line:84%
Or you would just
say, "T is open,"

00:03:13.640 --> 00:03:26.520 align:middle line:84%
which means for all open u, for
every open subset of B1, T of u

00:03:26.520 --> 00:03:31.030 align:middle line:90%
is open in B2.

00:03:31.030 --> 00:03:33.535 align:middle line:84%
So T takes open
sets to open sets.

00:03:33.535 --> 00:03:36.830 align:middle line:90%


00:03:36.830 --> 00:03:38.470 align:middle line:90%
So statement of the theorem--

00:03:38.470 --> 00:03:40.840 align:middle line:84%
if you have a bounded linear
operator between two Banach

00:03:40.840 --> 00:03:45.610 align:middle line:84%
spaces which is surjective,
then T is an open map.

00:03:45.610 --> 00:03:54.710 align:middle line:90%


00:03:54.710 --> 00:03:58.640 align:middle line:84%
So we're first
going to specialize

00:03:58.640 --> 00:04:02.120 align:middle line:90%
to one type of open set.

00:04:02.120 --> 00:04:12.782 align:middle line:84%
And we're going to prove a
specialized version of what

00:04:12.782 --> 00:04:13.490 align:middle line:90%
we want to prove.

00:04:13.490 --> 00:04:16.490 align:middle line:84%
And then we're going to show
using the linearity of T,

00:04:16.490 --> 00:04:18.589 align:middle line:84%
along with scaling
and translation,

00:04:18.589 --> 00:04:22.010 align:middle line:84%
that this, then, implies
that open sets get

00:04:22.010 --> 00:04:22.930 align:middle line:90%
mapped to open sets.

00:04:22.930 --> 00:04:25.420 align:middle line:84%
So first, what we're
going to prove is that--

00:04:25.420 --> 00:04:38.240 align:middle line:90%


00:04:38.240 --> 00:04:49.370 align:middle line:84%
let me recall this is the set
of all B in B1 such that--

00:04:49.370 --> 00:04:53.920 align:middle line:90%


00:04:53.920 --> 00:04:58.315 align:middle line:90%
so this is the open ball in B1--

00:04:58.315 --> 00:05:02.460 align:middle line:90%


00:05:02.460 --> 00:05:19.115 align:middle line:84%
then the image of this open
ball contains an open ball.

00:05:19.115 --> 00:05:24.800 align:middle line:90%


00:05:24.800 --> 00:05:33.335 align:middle line:84%
So now, the image is in B2, an
open ball in B2 centered at 0.

00:05:33.335 --> 00:05:37.920 align:middle line:90%


00:05:37.920 --> 00:05:44.580 align:middle line:84%
So T of the open ball
could be some of weird set,

00:05:44.580 --> 00:05:47.040 align:middle line:84%
and we know that linear
maps take 0 to 0.

00:05:47.040 --> 00:05:51.360 align:middle line:84%
What we're saying is that there
exists an open ball contained

00:05:51.360 --> 00:05:51.945 align:middle line:90%
in the image.

00:05:51.945 --> 00:05:54.920 align:middle line:90%


00:05:54.920 --> 00:05:57.595 align:middle line:84%
Now, like I said,
once we prove this,

00:05:57.595 --> 00:05:58.970 align:middle line:84%
we're going to
use linearity of T

00:05:58.970 --> 00:06:03.710 align:middle line:84%
to be able to shift these
balls around, shift and scale

00:06:03.710 --> 00:06:05.450 align:middle line:84%
these balls around
so that we prove

00:06:05.450 --> 00:06:08.880 align:middle line:90%
the result for every open u.

00:06:08.880 --> 00:06:11.270 align:middle line:90%
But this is the heart of it.

00:06:11.270 --> 00:06:18.780 align:middle line:90%


00:06:18.780 --> 00:06:28.330 align:middle line:84%
So since T is surjective,
everything in B2

00:06:28.330 --> 00:06:29.350 align:middle line:90%
gets mapped onto.

00:06:29.350 --> 00:06:32.890 align:middle line:90%


00:06:32.890 --> 00:06:38.530 align:middle line:84%
So B2 is equal to the
union over N natural number

00:06:38.530 --> 00:06:44.880 align:middle line:90%
of T of B closure.

00:06:44.880 --> 00:06:49.890 align:middle line:84%
So everything in B2 gets
mapped to by something in B1.

00:06:49.890 --> 00:06:58.320 align:middle line:84%
Everything in B1 lies in some
ball centered at 0 in B1.

00:06:58.320 --> 00:07:01.890 align:middle line:84%
Just take N bigger than
the norm of fixed guy,

00:07:01.890 --> 00:07:04.860 align:middle line:84%
and then its image will
be contained in this.

00:07:04.860 --> 00:07:08.040 align:middle line:84%
And we're taking closure
just so that we can write B2

00:07:08.040 --> 00:07:11.160 align:middle line:84%
as a union of closed
sets, so that we can now

00:07:11.160 --> 00:07:12.630 align:middle line:90%
apply Baire's theorem.

00:07:12.630 --> 00:07:16.430 align:middle line:90%


00:07:16.430 --> 00:07:26.880 align:middle line:84%
So this implies by Baire that
there exists a natural number

00:07:26.880 --> 00:07:41.470 align:middle line:84%
n0 such that the image of this
ball contains an interior point

00:07:41.470 --> 00:07:50.380 align:middle line:90%
or it contains an open ball.

00:07:50.380 --> 00:07:51.895 align:middle line:90%
Now, here's the thing--

00:07:51.895 --> 00:07:55.190 align:middle line:90%


00:07:55.190 --> 00:08:02.000 align:middle line:84%
T is linear so the image by T of
the ball of radius n0 centered

00:08:02.000 --> 00:08:03.650 align:middle line:90%
at 0 is the same as--

00:08:03.650 --> 00:08:13.170 align:middle line:90%


00:08:13.170 --> 00:08:17.370 align:middle line:84%
so what I'm saying is, I'm using
a little bit of handy notation

00:08:17.370 --> 00:08:19.380 align:middle line:84%
which I didn't
introduce yet, but if I

00:08:19.380 --> 00:08:26.910 align:middle line:84%
have a subset of a vector space
and I have some number outside

00:08:26.910 --> 00:08:30.210 align:middle line:84%
of it, what I mean by
this is the set obtained

00:08:30.210 --> 00:08:32.070 align:middle line:84%
by taking this set
and multiplying it

00:08:32.070 --> 00:08:37.179 align:middle line:84%
all by the scalar, which is
meaningful for vector space.

00:08:37.179 --> 00:08:42.429 align:middle line:84%
Now, by scaling, you can
check that this thing

00:08:42.429 --> 00:08:45.280 align:middle line:84%
under the closure is
the same as n0 times

00:08:45.280 --> 00:08:49.210 align:middle line:84%
T of the image of the
open ball centered at 1.

00:08:49.210 --> 00:08:52.750 align:middle line:84%
And then the closure's also
with respect to scaling.

00:08:52.750 --> 00:09:10.160 align:middle line:84%
So N0 times the closure
of the image of B 0, 1

00:09:10.160 --> 00:09:14.200 align:middle line:84%
contains an open ball so
let me just draw, again,

00:09:14.200 --> 00:09:16.850 align:middle line:90%
a picture to convince you.

00:09:16.850 --> 00:09:17.680 align:middle line:90%
So now I have--

00:09:17.680 --> 00:09:21.700 align:middle line:90%


00:09:21.700 --> 00:09:23.330 align:middle line:90%
let's draw it like this--

00:09:23.330 --> 00:09:24.300 align:middle line:90%
this is n0.

00:09:24.300 --> 00:09:29.400 align:middle line:90%


00:09:29.400 --> 00:09:39.620 align:middle line:84%
Now, if I take this set and
now multiply it by 1 over n0,

00:09:39.620 --> 00:09:51.420 align:middle line:84%
I just get the
rescaled guy, which is,

00:09:51.420 --> 00:09:58.320 align:middle line:84%
to use a fancy word,
homeomorphic to n0 times

00:09:58.320 --> 00:09:59.580 align:middle line:90%
the image of the open ball.

00:09:59.580 --> 00:10:03.750 align:middle line:90%


00:10:03.750 --> 00:10:05.490 align:middle line:84%
So this is just a
picture to back up

00:10:05.490 --> 00:10:08.190 align:middle line:84%
what I'm about to say next,
which you can also verify

00:10:08.190 --> 00:10:12.510 align:middle line:84%
not just by pictures but by
going through definitions,

00:10:12.510 --> 00:10:24.775 align:middle line:84%
which implies that T B0
contains an open ball.

00:10:24.775 --> 00:10:43.340 align:middle line:90%


00:10:43.340 --> 00:10:45.300 align:middle line:90%
So what's this mean?

00:10:45.300 --> 00:10:58.200 align:middle line:84%
This means there exists
some point B2 and a number r

00:10:58.200 --> 00:11:09.140 align:middle line:84%
positive such that the
ball of radius 4r--

00:11:09.140 --> 00:11:11.510 align:middle line:84%
so 4 just because
it's going to come in

00:11:11.510 --> 00:11:16.850 align:middle line:84%
handy later with arithmetic,
so just by choosing r small--

00:11:16.850 --> 00:11:26.180 align:middle line:84%
is contained in the
closure of the image

00:11:26.180 --> 00:11:29.680 align:middle line:90%
of the open ball of radius 1.

00:11:29.680 --> 00:11:38.550 align:middle line:90%


00:11:38.550 --> 00:11:46.750 align:middle line:84%
So this ball is
contained in the closure

00:11:46.750 --> 00:11:51.010 align:middle line:84%
and therefore, this is
contained in the closure.

00:11:51.010 --> 00:12:00.630 align:middle line:84%
And therefore, there
exists a point v1

00:12:00.630 --> 00:12:09.150 align:middle line:84%
which is equal to T
of u1, which is in T.

00:12:09.150 --> 00:12:13.890 align:middle line:84%
So v0 is in the closure,
so I can find points

00:12:13.890 --> 00:12:16.548 align:middle line:84%
from the image of
this ball close to it.

00:12:16.548 --> 00:12:18.090 align:middle line:84%
So that's just what
I'm writing here.

00:12:18.090 --> 00:12:23.400 align:middle line:84%
So there exists a v1 which
is the image of some guy

00:12:23.400 --> 00:12:36.590 align:middle line:84%
u1 in the open ball such
that it's close to v0.

00:12:36.590 --> 00:12:39.110 align:middle line:90%


00:12:39.110 --> 00:12:42.240 align:middle line:84%
And how close, so I
don't mess this up?

00:12:42.240 --> 00:12:42.740 align:middle line:90%
2r.

00:12:42.740 --> 00:12:51.190 align:middle line:90%


00:12:51.190 --> 00:12:57.470 align:middle line:90%
So why did I do that arithmetic?

00:12:57.470 --> 00:13:02.880 align:middle line:84%
Simply because if I look
at the ball of radius

00:13:02.880 --> 00:13:06.600 align:middle line:90%
2r centered at v1--

00:13:06.600 --> 00:13:12.870 align:middle line:84%
so v1 is within distance 2r
to v0, so everything in here

00:13:12.870 --> 00:13:16.860 align:middle line:84%
is going to be within
distance 4r to v0.

00:13:16.860 --> 00:13:21.090 align:middle line:84%
And since the ball of radius
4r is contained in here,

00:13:21.090 --> 00:13:21.900 align:middle line:90%
I obtain--

00:13:21.900 --> 00:13:24.310 align:middle line:90%


00:13:24.310 --> 00:13:26.060 align:middle line:84%
so this is what I just
said a minute ago--

00:13:26.060 --> 00:13:34.250 align:middle line:84%
this is contained in the ball of
the image of the ball of radius

00:13:34.250 --> 00:13:36.290 align:middle line:90%
1, the closure of that.

00:13:36.290 --> 00:13:43.910 align:middle line:90%


00:13:43.910 --> 00:13:46.610 align:middle line:84%
So now, what I'm going
to show is that--

00:13:46.610 --> 00:13:48.320 align:middle line:84%
I almost have what
I want to prove--

00:13:48.320 --> 00:13:54.200 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to show that the
closure contains an open ball.

00:13:54.200 --> 00:13:56.450 align:middle line:84%
That's not quite what I want
to show because remember,

00:13:56.450 --> 00:14:00.170 align:middle line:84%
I want to show that the
image of the ball of radius 1

00:14:00.170 --> 00:14:01.520 align:middle line:90%
contains an open ball.

00:14:01.520 --> 00:14:06.560 align:middle line:84%
But what I'm about to show
is that the closure of that

00:14:06.560 --> 00:14:09.470 align:middle line:90%
contains an open ball.

00:14:09.470 --> 00:14:22.540 align:middle line:84%
Now, if v is less than r,
then if I look at this element

00:14:22.540 --> 00:14:31.030 align:middle line:84%
1/2 times 2v plus
v1, this is in what?

00:14:31.030 --> 00:14:33.100 align:middle line:90%
This is in 1/2--

00:14:33.100 --> 00:14:38.560 align:middle line:84%
so 2v plus v1, 2v has
a norm less than 2r.

00:14:38.560 --> 00:14:44.140 align:middle line:84%
So this element is in the
ball the ball centered

00:14:44.140 --> 00:14:45.940 align:middle line:90%
at v1 of radius 2r.

00:14:45.940 --> 00:14:48.460 align:middle line:84%
So that's contained
in the closure.

00:14:48.460 --> 00:14:50.620 align:middle line:84%
So multiplying by
1/2, I get this

00:14:50.620 --> 00:15:06.690 align:middle line:84%
is closure, which, as I've
said, using the linearity of T

00:15:06.690 --> 00:15:10.650 align:middle line:84%
and the homogeneity
of the norm tells me

00:15:10.650 --> 00:15:14.430 align:middle line:84%
that this element
is in the closure

00:15:14.430 --> 00:15:19.930 align:middle line:84%
of the ball of the image
of the ball of radius 1/2.

00:15:19.930 --> 00:15:22.120 align:middle line:90%
So this is not crazy.

00:15:22.120 --> 00:15:24.670 align:middle line:84%
This is an element
of this, so 1/2

00:15:24.670 --> 00:15:28.400 align:middle line:84%
this ought to be an element
of 1/2 times this set.

00:15:28.400 --> 00:15:31.990 align:middle line:84%
And then the 1/2 comes all the
way through because T is linear

00:15:31.990 --> 00:15:37.730 align:middle line:84%
and the norm is homogeneous,
so the 1/2 can come through.

00:15:37.730 --> 00:15:45.095 align:middle line:84%
But as you're seeing this,
think it through slowly.

00:15:45.095 --> 00:15:50.350 align:middle line:90%


00:15:50.350 --> 00:15:57.190 align:middle line:84%
So that implies that v, which
I can write as minus T of u1

00:15:57.190 --> 00:15:58.160 align:middle line:90%
over 2.

00:15:58.160 --> 00:16:00.730 align:middle line:84%
u1 here was just
defined as an element

00:16:00.730 --> 00:16:03.130 align:middle line:84%
of the ball of radius
1, which gave me

00:16:03.130 --> 00:16:07.180 align:middle line:90%
v1, which was close to v0.

00:16:07.180 --> 00:16:11.140 align:middle line:84%
So let me keep up this
string of inequalities--

00:16:11.140 --> 00:16:15.865 align:middle line:90%
plus 1/2 times 2v plus v1.

00:16:15.865 --> 00:16:18.550 align:middle line:90%


00:16:18.550 --> 00:16:21.235 align:middle line:90%
Now, this is an element of--

00:16:21.235 --> 00:16:25.040 align:middle line:90%


00:16:25.040 --> 00:16:29.960 align:middle line:84%
don't confuse what I'm about
to write down with the notation

00:16:29.960 --> 00:16:33.860 align:middle line:84%
we used when we were
talking about quotients.

00:16:33.860 --> 00:16:35.240 align:middle line:90%
That's not what I mean here.

00:16:35.240 --> 00:16:38.870 align:middle line:84%
I mean take this set and add
this fixed element to it.

00:16:38.870 --> 00:16:41.520 align:middle line:90%


00:16:41.520 --> 00:16:43.760 align:middle line:84%
So this set here is
the set of all elements

00:16:43.760 --> 00:16:47.540 align:middle line:84%
of the form something in
here plus this fixed element.

00:16:47.540 --> 00:16:50.430 align:middle line:90%


00:16:50.430 --> 00:16:56.730 align:middle line:84%
And now again, by the
linearity of T this set

00:16:56.730 --> 00:17:04.260 align:middle line:84%
here is exactly the same
as T of minus u1 over 2

00:17:04.260 --> 00:17:10.890 align:middle line:84%
plus ball of radius 0 centered
at 1/2, with the closure

00:17:10.890 --> 00:17:11.670 align:middle line:90%
over all of that.

00:17:11.670 --> 00:17:15.089 align:middle line:84%
And the closure
respects everything

00:17:15.089 --> 00:17:16.170 align:middle line:90%
that we're doing here.

00:17:16.170 --> 00:17:19.460 align:middle line:90%


00:17:19.460 --> 00:17:26.060 align:middle line:84%
Now, u1 has radius or
has norm less than 1.

00:17:26.060 --> 00:17:29.480 align:middle line:84%
And therefore, u1 over 2
has norm less than 1/2.

00:17:29.480 --> 00:17:32.120 align:middle line:84%
Everything in here has
norm less than 1/2.

00:17:32.120 --> 00:17:36.140 align:middle line:84%
So something with norm less than
1/2 plus something with norm

00:17:36.140 --> 00:17:39.170 align:middle line:84%
less than 1/2 is something
with norm less than 1.

00:17:39.170 --> 00:17:51.420 align:middle line:84%
So this is contained in the
ball of radius 1 centered

00:17:51.420 --> 00:17:56.390 align:middle line:84%
at 0, and then closure,
and take the image of T.

00:17:56.390 --> 00:18:01.180 align:middle line:84%
So here, I'm using
that this is contained

00:18:01.180 --> 00:18:03.265 align:middle line:90%
in the ball of radius 1.

00:18:03.265 --> 00:18:11.290 align:middle line:90%


00:18:11.290 --> 00:18:13.390 align:middle line:84%
So that's almost what
we wanted to show.

00:18:13.390 --> 00:18:18.720 align:middle line:84%
What we have shown is
that the ball of radius r

00:18:18.720 --> 00:18:24.330 align:middle line:84%
is contained in the closure
of the ball of radius

00:18:24.330 --> 00:18:28.275 align:middle line:84%
1, the closure of the image
of the ball of radius 1.

00:18:28.275 --> 00:18:36.920 align:middle line:90%


00:18:36.920 --> 00:18:44.500 align:middle line:84%
Now, again, by scaling
this, let me just--

00:18:44.500 --> 00:18:56.780 align:middle line:90%


00:18:56.780 --> 00:19:00.580 align:middle line:90%
now, the ball of radius r is--

00:19:00.580 --> 00:19:03.590 align:middle line:90%


00:19:03.590 --> 00:19:06.410 align:middle line:84%
so let me just write down
what I'm going to write down.

00:19:06.410 --> 00:19:09.030 align:middle line:90%
Then I'll explain it.

00:19:09.030 --> 00:19:13.770 align:middle line:84%
And therefore, the ball
of radius 2 to the minus

00:19:13.770 --> 00:19:21.390 align:middle line:84%
n times r, which is equal to the
set of elements of the form 2

00:19:21.390 --> 00:19:25.470 align:middle line:84%
to the minus n times
the ball of radius--

00:19:25.470 --> 00:19:30.945 align:middle line:84%
elements in the ball of
radius r is contained in 2

00:19:30.945 --> 00:19:39.290 align:middle line:90%
to the minus n, which equals.

00:19:39.290 --> 00:19:47.220 align:middle line:90%


00:19:47.220 --> 00:19:51.620 align:middle line:84%
So all I did right
here was say, again,

00:19:51.620 --> 00:19:57.620 align:middle line:84%
by homogeneity of the
norm, the ball of radius

00:19:57.620 --> 00:20:01.040 align:middle line:84%
r or the ball of radius
2 to the minus n times r

00:20:01.040 --> 00:20:04.760 align:middle line:84%
is the same as 2 to the
minus n times all elements

00:20:04.760 --> 00:20:07.130 align:middle line:90%
of the ball of radius r.

00:20:07.130 --> 00:20:11.450 align:middle line:84%
And that's contained in this
by what we've already proven.

00:20:11.450 --> 00:20:15.350 align:middle line:84%
So this is contained in the set
of all elements of the form 2

00:20:15.350 --> 00:20:19.550 align:middle line:84%
to the minus n times
things from this set.

00:20:19.550 --> 00:20:22.190 align:middle line:84%
And again, by homogeneity,
2 to the minus n

00:20:22.190 --> 00:20:29.950 align:middle line:84%
comes through here
and then through here

00:20:29.950 --> 00:20:33.190 align:middle line:90%
for all n in natural numbers.

00:20:33.190 --> 00:20:36.030 align:middle line:90%


00:20:36.030 --> 00:20:37.625 align:middle line:90%
So let me put--

00:20:37.625 --> 00:20:41.750 align:middle line:90%


00:20:41.750 --> 00:20:49.480 align:middle line:84%
what I've proven
is this and that.

00:20:49.480 --> 00:20:56.180 align:middle line:84%
So that's almost what I wanted
to prove right here, just

00:20:56.180 --> 00:20:57.823 align:middle line:84%
I want to be able
to drop the closure.

00:20:57.823 --> 00:20:59.240 align:middle line:84%
And now what I'm
going to prove is

00:20:59.240 --> 00:21:03.260 align:middle line:84%
that I can drop the
closure if I take

00:21:03.260 --> 00:21:06.920 align:middle line:90%
the ball of radius r over 2.

00:21:06.920 --> 00:21:18.650 align:middle line:84%
So now, we'll prove that
the ball of radius r over 2

00:21:18.650 --> 00:21:29.600 align:middle line:84%
is, in fact, contained in the
image of the unit ball by T.

00:21:29.600 --> 00:21:33.980 align:middle line:84%
Now, we don't have that
immediately because we

00:21:33.980 --> 00:21:36.680 align:middle line:84%
have the ball of radius
1/2 r is contained

00:21:36.680 --> 00:21:40.280 align:middle line:84%
in the closure of the
image of T of this.

00:21:40.280 --> 00:21:42.620 align:middle line:84%
There's nothing that
doesn't say that the closure

00:21:42.620 --> 00:21:45.755 align:middle line:84%
of the image of this
thing could end up being--

00:21:45.755 --> 00:21:48.680 align:middle line:90%


00:21:48.680 --> 00:21:53.240 align:middle line:84%
there's nothing that says
that that has to be contained

00:21:53.240 --> 00:21:54.530 align:middle line:90%
strictly within this ball.

00:21:54.530 --> 00:22:13.610 align:middle line:90%


00:22:13.610 --> 00:22:17.580 align:middle line:90%
So let's show this.

00:22:17.580 --> 00:22:22.350 align:middle line:84%
Let v have norm less than r
over 2, so it's in this ball.

00:22:22.350 --> 00:22:27.960 align:middle line:84%
Then, as we've
proven up here, v is

00:22:27.960 --> 00:22:35.220 align:middle line:84%
in the image of by T of
the ball of radius 1/2.

00:22:35.220 --> 00:22:40.430 align:middle line:90%


00:22:40.430 --> 00:22:52.370 align:middle line:84%
And therefore, there exists
a b1 in the ball of 0,

00:22:52.370 --> 00:23:08.580 align:middle line:84%
radius 1/2 such that the norm
of v minus T of b1 is less than,

00:23:08.580 --> 00:23:14.700 align:middle line:90%
let's say, r over 4.

00:23:14.700 --> 00:23:18.930 align:middle line:90%


00:23:18.930 --> 00:23:21.190 align:middle line:84%
And now we're going
to iterate this.

00:23:21.190 --> 00:23:25.140 align:middle line:84%
So now, think of this element
as lying in the ball of radius

00:23:25.140 --> 00:23:25.890 align:middle line:90%
r over 4.

00:23:25.890 --> 00:23:31.000 align:middle line:90%


00:23:31.000 --> 00:23:33.970 align:middle line:84%
And therefore, now I
take n equals 2 in here.

00:23:33.970 --> 00:23:43.970 align:middle line:84%
Thus, v minus T b1
is in the closure

00:23:43.970 --> 00:23:48.980 align:middle line:84%
of the image of the
ball of radius of 1/4,

00:23:48.980 --> 00:24:00.860 align:middle line:84%
which implies there exists a
v2 in the ball of radius 4 such

00:24:00.860 --> 00:24:10.510 align:middle line:84%
that v minus T b1 minus T
b2 norm is less than half

00:24:10.510 --> 00:24:14.260 align:middle line:84%
of what we had
before, so r over 8.

00:24:14.260 --> 00:24:18.430 align:middle line:84%
But now you see the
game that we're playing.

00:24:18.430 --> 00:24:20.320 align:middle line:84%
This is now in
the ball of radius

00:24:20.320 --> 00:24:24.460 align:middle line:84%
r over 8, which is
implies this is contained

00:24:24.460 --> 00:24:30.370 align:middle line:84%
in the closure of the
image of the ball centered

00:24:30.370 --> 00:24:32.410 align:middle line:90%
at 0 of radius 1 over 8.

00:24:32.410 --> 00:24:37.330 align:middle line:84%
And therefore, I can find a
b3 in the ball of radius 1

00:24:37.330 --> 00:24:42.490 align:middle line:84%
over 8 so that v minus T
b1 minus T b2 minus T b3

00:24:42.490 --> 00:24:44.380 align:middle line:90%
is less than r over 16.

00:24:44.380 --> 00:24:47.380 align:middle line:84%
And then, we just
continue inductively.

00:24:47.380 --> 00:24:57.160 align:middle line:84%
So I said I would do the
proper induction argument once

00:24:57.160 --> 00:24:58.213 align:middle line:90%
and I did it last time.

00:24:58.213 --> 00:24:59.380 align:middle line:90%
So I will never do it again.

00:24:59.380 --> 00:25:21.880 align:middle line:84%
Continuing, we obtained a
sequence b k of elements in B1

00:25:21.880 --> 00:25:32.975 align:middle line:84%
such that the norm of b k is
less than 2 to the minus k.

00:25:32.975 --> 00:25:35.540 align:middle line:90%


00:25:35.540 --> 00:25:39.370 align:middle line:90%
And so there's that.

00:25:39.370 --> 00:25:48.960 align:middle line:84%
And if I look at v minus sum
from k equals 1 to n T of b k,

00:25:48.960 --> 00:25:57.620 align:middle line:84%
this is less than 2 to the
minus n minus 1 times r.

00:25:57.620 --> 00:25:59.570 align:middle line:90%
Let me make sure my--

00:25:59.570 --> 00:26:00.070 align:middle line:90%
yeah.

00:26:00.070 --> 00:26:10.740 align:middle line:90%


00:26:10.740 --> 00:26:13.080 align:middle line:84%
Now, we've used the fact
that B2 is complete.

00:26:13.080 --> 00:26:15.405 align:middle line:84%
We haven't used the fact
that B1 is complete yet.

00:26:15.405 --> 00:26:19.090 align:middle line:90%


00:26:19.090 --> 00:26:21.750 align:middle line:84%
So we ought to use
it at some point.

00:26:21.750 --> 00:26:23.280 align:middle line:84%
We're going to use
it now by showing

00:26:23.280 --> 00:26:29.310 align:middle line:84%
that v has to be, in fact,
inside the image of the radius

00:26:29.310 --> 00:26:31.050 align:middle line:90%
of the ball of radius 1.

00:26:31.050 --> 00:26:38.890 align:middle line:84%
So these b k's form
a Cauchy sequence--

00:26:38.890 --> 00:26:42.770 align:middle line:90%


00:26:42.770 --> 00:26:44.810 align:middle line:84%
or I shouldn't say they
form a Cauchy sequence,

00:26:44.810 --> 00:26:46.775 align:middle line:84%
but their sum forms
a Cauchy sequence.

00:26:46.775 --> 00:26:49.290 align:middle line:90%


00:26:49.290 --> 00:26:50.570 align:middle line:90%
Let me write it this way.

00:26:50.570 --> 00:27:03.880 align:middle line:90%


00:27:03.880 --> 00:27:11.610 align:middle line:84%
The series is
absolutely summable

00:27:11.610 --> 00:27:13.740 align:middle line:84%
because the norm is
bounded by 2 to the minus

00:27:13.740 --> 00:27:17.280 align:middle line:90%
k, which is summable.

00:27:17.280 --> 00:27:21.600 align:middle line:84%
And since B1 is a
Banach space, the series

00:27:21.600 --> 00:27:27.790 align:middle line:84%
must be summable, which implies
there exist a B and B1 such

00:27:27.790 --> 00:27:34.890 align:middle line:84%
that B is equal to the sum k
equals 1 to infinity of b k.

00:27:34.890 --> 00:27:42.910 align:middle line:90%


00:27:42.910 --> 00:27:51.180 align:middle line:84%
Moreover, the norm of b,
which is equal to the limit

00:27:51.180 --> 00:28:01.170 align:middle line:84%
as n goes to infinity of the
norm of n k equals 1 b k,

00:28:01.170 --> 00:28:08.380 align:middle line:84%
this is, by the
triangle inequality,

00:28:08.380 --> 00:28:14.590 align:middle line:84%
less than or equal to the limit
as n goes to infinity of--

00:28:14.590 --> 00:28:24.060 align:middle line:84%
equals 1 to n b k, which is
less than-- well, is equal to--

00:28:24.060 --> 00:28:28.620 align:middle line:84%
sum from k equals 1 to
infinity of the norm of b k.

00:28:28.620 --> 00:28:33.660 align:middle line:84%
And each of these is less
than 2 to the minus k.

00:28:33.660 --> 00:28:37.590 align:middle line:84%
So k equals 1 to infinity
of 2 to the minus k.

00:28:37.590 --> 00:28:39.405 align:middle line:90%
And that sum is just 1.

00:28:39.405 --> 00:28:43.100 align:middle line:90%


00:28:43.100 --> 00:28:51.080 align:middle line:84%
So the norm of b is less than
1 using the first property

00:28:51.080 --> 00:28:52.850 align:middle line:90%
of this sequence.

00:28:52.850 --> 00:28:54.710 align:middle line:84%
The second property
of this sequence

00:28:54.710 --> 00:29:04.240 align:middle line:84%
then shows that v is equal
to the image of b by T.

00:29:04.240 --> 00:29:17.520 align:middle line:84%
Moreover, since T
is continuous, T b,

00:29:17.520 --> 00:29:21.410 align:middle line:84%
which is equal to
the limit as n goes

00:29:21.410 --> 00:29:29.950 align:middle line:84%
to infinity of t applied
to k equals 1 to n b k,

00:29:29.950 --> 00:29:32.950 align:middle line:84%
which equals, by
linearity, limit

00:29:32.950 --> 00:29:42.180 align:middle line:84%
as n goes to infinity of sum
from k equals 1 to n T of b k.

00:29:42.180 --> 00:29:46.210 align:middle line:90%


00:29:46.210 --> 00:29:50.230 align:middle line:84%
By the second property,
the limit as n

00:29:50.230 --> 00:29:58.210 align:middle line:84%
goes to infinity of T of
b sub k is equal to v.

00:29:58.210 --> 00:30:11.200 align:middle line:84%
And therefore, B is in the image
of the ball of radius 1 by T.

00:30:11.200 --> 00:30:19.550 align:middle line:84%
And thus, the ball
of radius r over 2

00:30:19.550 --> 00:30:24.770 align:middle line:84%
is contained in the image
of the ball of radius 1.

00:30:24.770 --> 00:30:27.630 align:middle line:90%


00:30:27.630 --> 00:30:34.220 align:middle line:84%
So that's the special-ish
case that I wanted

00:30:34.220 --> 00:30:35.760 align:middle line:90%
to prove in terms of open sets.

00:30:35.760 --> 00:30:41.860 align:middle line:84%
So what I've shown
is that, if you like,

00:30:41.860 --> 00:30:46.180 align:middle line:84%
the interior point 0
remains an interior point.

00:30:46.180 --> 00:30:49.375 align:middle line:84%
Now let's show that implies
the full claim of what

00:30:49.375 --> 00:30:51.250 align:middle line:84%
I want to prove for the
open mapping theorem,

00:30:51.250 --> 00:30:56.260 align:middle line:84%
that every open set gets
mapped to an open set in B2.

00:30:56.260 --> 00:30:58.760 align:middle line:90%
We'll just use translation.

00:30:58.760 --> 00:31:15.300 align:middle line:84%
So in scaling again, suppose
u subset of B1 is open and B2

00:31:15.300 --> 00:31:21.660 align:middle line:84%
is the image of something
in u, so this is in T of u.

00:31:21.660 --> 00:31:28.670 align:middle line:90%


00:31:28.670 --> 00:31:35.570 align:middle line:84%
Then there exists an epsilon
positive such that B1--

00:31:35.570 --> 00:31:36.935 align:middle line:90%
so remember, u is open--

00:31:36.935 --> 00:31:40.200 align:middle line:90%


00:31:40.200 --> 00:31:47.770 align:middle line:84%
so B1 plus all elements
of the ball centered

00:31:47.770 --> 00:31:54.720 align:middle line:84%
at 0 of radius epsilon
and this, which

00:31:54.720 --> 00:31:59.340 align:middle line:84%
is equal to the ball centered
at B1 of radius epsilon

00:31:59.340 --> 00:32:01.905 align:middle line:84%
is contained in u
since it was open.

00:32:01.905 --> 00:32:07.620 align:middle line:90%


00:32:07.620 --> 00:32:14.540 align:middle line:84%
Now, since there
exists a delta positive

00:32:14.540 --> 00:32:20.480 align:middle line:84%
such that ball of radius
delta is contained

00:32:20.480 --> 00:32:28.930 align:middle line:84%
in the ball of radius
0, 1, this implies

00:32:28.930 --> 00:32:32.680 align:middle line:90%
that the ball of radius--

00:32:32.680 --> 00:32:37.510 align:middle line:84%
again, by homogeneity, the ball
of radius epsilon times delta

00:32:37.510 --> 00:32:46.620 align:middle line:84%
is contained in the image of
the ball of radius epsilon,

00:32:46.620 --> 00:32:51.290 align:middle line:84%
again, because T is linear
and the norm is homogeneous.

00:32:51.290 --> 00:33:05.110 align:middle line:90%


00:33:05.110 --> 00:33:08.860 align:middle line:84%
So let's go back to how
we had it written down.

00:33:08.860 --> 00:33:21.390 align:middle line:84%
So b2 plus-- so this is
the of epsilon times delta.

00:33:21.390 --> 00:33:23.640 align:middle line:84%
I got my delta and
epsilons backwards.

00:33:23.640 --> 00:33:28.110 align:middle line:84%
Anyways, this is equal
to b2 plus epsilon

00:33:28.110 --> 00:33:32.010 align:middle line:90%
times the ball of radius delta.

00:33:32.010 --> 00:33:37.230 align:middle line:84%
So this is actually equal to
the b2 plus the ball of radius 0

00:33:37.230 --> 00:33:39.690 align:middle line:84%
centered at 0 of radius
epsilon times delta.

00:33:39.690 --> 00:33:41.895 align:middle line:90%
The epsilon I can pull out.

00:33:41.895 --> 00:33:47.930 align:middle line:90%


00:33:47.930 --> 00:33:55.580 align:middle line:84%
Now, this is contained
in b2 plus epsilon

00:33:55.580 --> 00:34:02.480 align:middle line:90%
times the ball of radius 0, 1.

00:34:02.480 --> 00:34:05.920 align:middle line:84%
Yeah, I don't know
why I wrote this down.

00:34:05.920 --> 00:34:07.900 align:middle line:90%
I don't think I needed it.

00:34:07.900 --> 00:34:11.650 align:middle line:90%
Anyways, OK.

00:34:11.650 --> 00:34:20.290 align:middle line:84%
And this is equal to T of
b1 plus epsilon T of B 0, 1.

00:34:20.290 --> 00:34:23.750 align:middle line:84%
And again, by linearity
and homogeneity,

00:34:23.750 --> 00:34:31.150 align:middle line:84%
this is equal to the image
of b1 plus epsilon ball

00:34:31.150 --> 00:34:44.400 align:middle line:84%
of radius 1, which I can say is
b1 plus ball of radius epsilon.

00:34:44.400 --> 00:34:49.580 align:middle line:84%
Now, this epsilon, remember, was
chosen so that this ball here

00:34:49.580 --> 00:34:51.170 align:middle line:90%
is contained within u.

00:34:51.170 --> 00:34:54.679 align:middle line:84%
And therefore, this
is contained within u

00:34:54.679 --> 00:34:59.330 align:middle line:84%
and the image is, therefore,
contained in the image of u.

00:34:59.330 --> 00:35:04.370 align:middle line:84%
Yes, so I'm not sure why I
decided to write this down.

00:35:04.370 --> 00:35:05.920 align:middle line:84%
This is what threw
me for a loop.

00:35:05.920 --> 00:35:10.000 align:middle line:84%
But anyways, so
this is the point--

00:35:10.000 --> 00:35:12.310 align:middle line:84%
that we can just
take the special case

00:35:12.310 --> 00:35:17.500 align:middle line:84%
and shift things around to
get the general statement

00:35:17.500 --> 00:35:19.540 align:middle line:84%
for open sets getting
mapped to open sets.

00:35:19.540 --> 00:35:40.890 align:middle line:90%


00:35:40.890 --> 00:35:43.185 align:middle line:84%
So from the open
mapping theorem--

00:35:43.185 --> 00:35:48.520 align:middle line:90%


00:35:48.520 --> 00:35:50.410 align:middle line:84%
I don't know, it seems
almost topological--

00:35:50.410 --> 00:35:55.180 align:middle line:84%
but we get what's called the
closed graph theorem, which

00:35:55.180 --> 00:35:59.290 align:middle line:84%
gives you sufficient
conditions to be able to check

00:35:59.290 --> 00:36:00.710 align:middle line:90%
if something is continuous.

00:36:00.710 --> 00:36:02.697 align:middle line:84%
It's a little bit
more convenient.

00:36:02.697 --> 00:36:04.280 align:middle line:84%
And I'll explain why
in just a second.

00:36:04.280 --> 00:36:11.135 align:middle line:84%
So this is the
closed graph theorem.

00:36:11.135 --> 00:36:13.660 align:middle line:90%


00:36:13.660 --> 00:36:21.683 align:middle line:84%
But first, I need to state
just a simple theorem

00:36:21.683 --> 00:36:23.725 align:middle line:84%
before I actually state
the closed graph theorem.

00:36:23.725 --> 00:36:26.530 align:middle line:90%


00:36:26.530 --> 00:36:37.540 align:middle line:84%
If B1 and B2 are Banach spaces,
then their Cartesian product,

00:36:37.540 --> 00:36:42.280 align:middle line:84%
which I can give a natural
vector space structure

00:36:42.280 --> 00:36:47.770 align:middle line:84%
on from B1 and B2, just the sum
of an ordered pair of elements

00:36:47.770 --> 00:36:49.450 align:middle line:90%
is just the entry by entry sum.

00:36:49.450 --> 00:36:53.130 align:middle line:90%


00:36:53.130 --> 00:37:02.560 align:middle line:84%
But I can also put a norm
on it coming from these two,

00:37:02.560 --> 00:37:03.580 align:middle line:90%
with norm--

00:37:03.580 --> 00:37:09.490 align:middle line:84%
so for an ordered pair b1
and b2, the norm of this

00:37:09.490 --> 00:37:16.840 align:middle line:84%
is just defined to be
the sum of the norms

00:37:16.840 --> 00:37:18.730 align:middle line:90%
in the respective spaces.

00:37:18.730 --> 00:37:22.430 align:middle line:90%


00:37:22.430 --> 00:37:24.220 align:middle line:90%
So this is a norm space.

00:37:24.220 --> 00:37:29.110 align:middle line:84%
But moreover, if they're
both Banach spaces,

00:37:29.110 --> 00:37:30.220 align:middle line:90%
this is a Banach space.

00:37:30.220 --> 00:37:36.880 align:middle line:84%
So it's not difficult to prove
just based on the definition.

00:37:36.880 --> 00:37:44.065 align:middle line:90%


00:37:44.065 --> 00:37:45.440 align:middle line:84%
I'm not going to
write the proof.

00:37:45.440 --> 00:37:51.990 align:middle line:84%
I will leave it
to you, the proof.

00:37:51.990 --> 00:37:55.740 align:middle line:84%
Again, it's not
difficult. Simply

00:37:55.740 --> 00:37:59.310 align:middle line:84%
from how we've defined the
norm a Cauchy sequence in B1

00:37:59.310 --> 00:38:05.790 align:middle line:84%
cross B2, the first entry will
form a Cauchy sequence because

00:38:05.790 --> 00:38:09.750 align:middle line:84%
of this definition of
norm, and the second entry

00:38:09.750 --> 00:38:12.480 align:middle line:84%
of the sequence will form
a Cauchy sequence in B2.

00:38:12.480 --> 00:38:14.110 align:middle line:90%
Both of those have limits.

00:38:14.110 --> 00:38:19.740 align:middle line:84%
So then you can prove the
sequence consisting of

00:38:19.740 --> 00:38:21.780 align:middle line:90%
ordered pairs has a limit.

00:38:21.780 --> 00:38:24.960 align:middle line:84%
It's the same way you can
prove that R2 is complete

00:38:24.960 --> 00:38:26.280 align:middle line:90%
assuming R1 is complete.

00:38:26.280 --> 00:38:29.090 align:middle line:90%


00:38:29.090 --> 00:38:39.270 align:middle line:84%
All right, so now, I can state
the closed graph theorem,

00:38:39.270 --> 00:38:42.740 align:middle line:90%
which is the following--

00:38:42.740 --> 00:38:54.380 align:middle line:84%
if B1, B2 are Banach spaces
and you have a linear operator

00:38:54.380 --> 00:38:56.390 align:middle line:90%
from B1 to B2--

00:38:56.390 --> 00:39:06.495 align:middle line:84%
so all you know is it's linear,
don't know that it's bounded--

00:39:06.495 --> 00:39:10.070 align:middle line:90%


00:39:10.070 --> 00:39:12.110 align:middle line:84%
then there's an
equivalent condition

00:39:12.110 --> 00:39:14.880 align:middle line:84%
you can check to see
if it is, in fact,

00:39:14.880 --> 00:39:16.370 align:middle line:90%
a bounded linear operator.

00:39:16.370 --> 00:39:21.090 align:middle line:90%


00:39:21.090 --> 00:39:25.950 align:middle line:84%
Then T is a bounded
linear operator

00:39:25.950 --> 00:39:32.190 align:middle line:84%
from B1 to B2 if and
only if the graph of T

00:39:32.190 --> 00:39:36.000 align:middle line:90%
which is defined to be the set--

00:39:36.000 --> 00:39:39.300 align:middle line:84%
let's see, what
notation do I use--

00:39:39.300 --> 00:39:46.200 align:middle line:90%
u, v such that v equals T u--

00:39:46.200 --> 00:39:47.400 align:middle line:90%
let me write it this way--

00:39:47.400 --> 00:39:56.540 align:middle line:90%


00:39:56.540 --> 00:40:01.510 align:middle line:84%
which is a subset of
B1 cross B2, is closed.

00:40:01.510 --> 00:40:11.300 align:middle line:90%


00:40:11.300 --> 00:40:14.990 align:middle line:84%
So a linear operator is
a bounded linear operator

00:40:14.990 --> 00:40:21.140 align:middle line:84%
if and only if the graph of
this linear operator given by u,

00:40:21.140 --> 00:40:23.310 align:middle line:90%
T u is closed.

00:40:23.310 --> 00:40:26.690 align:middle line:84%
Now, why is this a
little bit easier

00:40:26.690 --> 00:40:30.450 align:middle line:84%
or I say convenient than
just checking if something's

00:40:30.450 --> 00:40:33.910 align:middle line:90%
a bounded linear operator?

00:40:33.910 --> 00:40:40.660 align:middle line:84%
Well, maybe it's difficult to
prove the boundedness property

00:40:40.660 --> 00:40:42.790 align:middle line:84%
that we have that's
equivalent to continuity.

00:40:42.790 --> 00:40:46.220 align:middle line:90%


00:40:46.220 --> 00:40:50.640 align:middle line:84%
So a bounded linear operator
is a linear operator

00:40:50.640 --> 00:40:51.480 align:middle line:90%
which is continuous.

00:40:51.480 --> 00:40:54.300 align:middle line:84%
So maybe it's difficult
to prove the bound.

00:40:54.300 --> 00:40:56.480 align:middle line:84%
So you have to go back
and try and prove--

00:40:56.480 --> 00:41:02.980 align:middle line:84%
just for a God-given or
instructor-given operator--

00:41:02.980 --> 00:41:05.920 align:middle line:84%
then you try and go back
and prove continuity.

00:41:05.920 --> 00:41:11.170 align:middle line:84%
And continuity says, well, take
a sequence u n's converging

00:41:11.170 --> 00:41:12.240 align:middle line:90%
to u.

00:41:12.240 --> 00:41:21.590 align:middle line:84%
You then have to prove that
T of u n converges to T of u.

00:41:21.590 --> 00:41:23.930 align:middle line:84%
But there's kind of two
statements in there.

00:41:23.930 --> 00:41:29.180 align:middle line:84%
You have to prove
T of u N converges

00:41:29.180 --> 00:41:32.900 align:middle line:90%
and that limit is equal to T u.

00:41:32.900 --> 00:41:34.750 align:middle line:84%
What the closed
graph theorem does

00:41:34.750 --> 00:41:37.340 align:middle line:84%
it eliminates one
of those steps.

00:41:37.340 --> 00:41:39.440 align:middle line:90%
Because to prove that the--

00:41:39.440 --> 00:41:43.760 align:middle line:84%
let's think about that, that the
graph is closed in B1 cross B2.

00:41:43.760 --> 00:41:44.910 align:middle line:90%
What does that mean?

00:41:44.910 --> 00:41:48.620 align:middle line:84%
That means you have to check
that it's closed under taking

00:41:48.620 --> 00:41:50.090 align:middle line:90%
limits of sequences.

00:41:50.090 --> 00:41:54.230 align:middle line:84%
So you have to show that given
a sequence u n tending to u

00:41:54.230 --> 00:41:59.090 align:middle line:84%
and T of u n converging to
v, that v is equal to T u.

00:41:59.090 --> 00:42:03.440 align:middle line:84%
So you get to assume already
that T of u n converges.

00:42:03.440 --> 00:42:07.310 align:middle line:84%
You just now have to check that
the thing it converges to is

00:42:07.310 --> 00:42:12.650 align:middle line:84%
actually equal to the image
of the limit of the u n's.

00:42:12.650 --> 00:42:18.710 align:middle line:84%
I hope that makes
sense and explains

00:42:18.710 --> 00:42:20.810 align:middle line:84%
why I said this is
actually a little more

00:42:20.810 --> 00:42:26.105 align:middle line:84%
convenient than continuity,
or at least useful.

00:42:26.105 --> 00:42:29.390 align:middle line:90%


00:42:29.390 --> 00:42:33.590 align:middle line:90%
So this is a two-way street.

00:42:33.590 --> 00:42:37.010 align:middle line:84%
There's always,
typically, one side

00:42:37.010 --> 00:42:38.450 align:middle line:90%
of the street that's plowed.

00:42:38.450 --> 00:42:43.280 align:middle line:84%
So let's do this
direction, assuming

00:42:43.280 --> 00:42:45.170 align:middle line:84%
that T is a bounded
linear operator,

00:42:45.170 --> 00:42:46.820 align:middle line:84%
and show that the
graph is closed.

00:42:46.820 --> 00:42:49.370 align:middle line:90%


00:42:49.370 --> 00:42:53.890 align:middle line:84%
So if T is a bounded
linear operator--

00:42:53.890 --> 00:43:00.740 align:middle line:90%


00:43:00.740 --> 00:43:03.935 align:middle line:84%
let me just write "suppose"
and then start a new sentence.

00:43:03.935 --> 00:43:08.390 align:middle line:90%


00:43:08.390 --> 00:43:27.990 align:middle line:84%
So let u n T of u n be a
sequence in the graph of T

00:43:27.990 --> 00:43:35.040 align:middle line:84%
such that u n converges to
u and T of u n converges

00:43:35.040 --> 00:43:39.260 align:middle line:84%
to v. To show the
graph is closed,

00:43:39.260 --> 00:43:44.780 align:middle line:84%
we have to show that that the
pair u, v is in the graph,

00:43:44.780 --> 00:43:47.030 align:middle line:90%
that v is equal to T of u.

00:43:47.030 --> 00:43:50.120 align:middle line:84%
But this follows
from continuity.

00:43:50.120 --> 00:43:58.175 align:middle line:84%
Then v is equal to the limit as
n goes to infinity of T of u n.

00:43:58.175 --> 00:44:00.230 align:middle line:84%
And since T is
continuous, this is

00:44:00.230 --> 00:44:08.485 align:middle line:84%
equal to [INAUDIBLE]
u n, which equals T u.

00:44:08.485 --> 00:44:11.490 align:middle line:90%


00:44:11.490 --> 00:44:24.930 align:middle line:84%
Thus, the ordered pair
u, v is in the graph.

00:44:24.930 --> 00:44:31.370 align:middle line:84%
So let's now prove the
opposite direction.

00:44:31.370 --> 00:44:50.240 align:middle line:84%
It's still not that difficult.
So what I'm first going to do

00:44:50.240 --> 00:44:53.530 align:middle line:90%
is I'm going to draw a diagram.

00:44:53.530 --> 00:44:56.570 align:middle line:84%
This may be the only diagram
I ever draw in this--

00:44:56.570 --> 00:45:03.625 align:middle line:84%
a commuting diagram-- maybe the
only one I draw in this class.

00:45:03.625 --> 00:45:04.125 align:middle line:90%
We'll see.

00:45:04.125 --> 00:45:07.650 align:middle line:90%


00:45:07.650 --> 00:45:12.730 align:middle line:90%
So T takes B1 to B2.

00:45:12.730 --> 00:45:16.920 align:middle line:84%
I have the graph
of T. And now, I'm

00:45:16.920 --> 00:45:20.400 align:middle line:84%
going to define two maps
going from the graph

00:45:20.400 --> 00:45:24.570 align:middle line:90%
to B1 and from the graph to B2.

00:45:24.570 --> 00:45:31.170 align:middle line:84%
The graph sits as a
subset of B1 cross B2.

00:45:31.170 --> 00:45:35.310 align:middle line:84%
So this first map
going from the graph

00:45:35.310 --> 00:45:37.230 align:middle line:84%
is just going to
be the projection

00:45:37.230 --> 00:45:39.210 align:middle line:90%
onto the first entry.

00:45:39.210 --> 00:45:46.665 align:middle line:84%
And pi 1 and then pi 2 will
be the projection onto pi 2.

00:45:46.665 --> 00:45:49.410 align:middle line:90%


00:45:49.410 --> 00:45:51.270 align:middle line:84%
Now, to finish
this graph, I need

00:45:51.270 --> 00:45:58.415 align:middle line:84%
to have an arrow going
from B1 up to gamma of T.

00:45:58.415 --> 00:45:59.665 align:middle line:90%
So I just first want to note--

00:45:59.665 --> 00:46:07.250 align:middle line:90%


00:46:07.250 --> 00:46:08.750 align:middle line:84%
actually before I
note it, I'm going

00:46:08.750 --> 00:46:09.650 align:middle line:90%
to go ahead and draw the arrow.

00:46:09.650 --> 00:46:11.060 align:middle line:84%
And then I'll tell
you what S is.

00:46:11.060 --> 00:46:14.930 align:middle line:90%


00:46:14.930 --> 00:46:22.310 align:middle line:84%
So pi 1 with respect
to the graph of T--

00:46:22.310 --> 00:46:30.840 align:middle line:90%
this is a surjective map.

00:46:30.840 --> 00:46:34.605 align:middle line:84%
So let me actually
define these things.

00:46:34.605 --> 00:46:42.130 align:middle line:90%


00:46:42.130 --> 00:46:49.480 align:middle line:84%
A graph of T to B1 via pi 1 of
an element of the form u, T u

00:46:49.480 --> 00:46:59.010 align:middle line:84%
equals u, and then pi 2
from the graph of T to B2

00:46:59.010 --> 00:47:05.850 align:middle line:84%
is just take the second
element, u T u equals T u.

00:47:05.850 --> 00:47:12.800 align:middle line:90%


00:47:12.800 --> 00:47:16.460 align:middle line:90%
So my point here--

00:47:16.460 --> 00:47:29.640 align:middle line:84%
so first note-- gamma
of T is a Banach space.

00:47:29.640 --> 00:47:30.630 align:middle line:90%
Why?

00:47:30.630 --> 00:47:38.080 align:middle line:84%
Gamma of t is a subspace of B1
cross B2 because T is linear

00:47:38.080 --> 00:47:39.340 align:middle line:90%
and it's closed.

00:47:39.340 --> 00:47:42.160 align:middle line:84%
So a closed subspace
of a Banach space

00:47:42.160 --> 00:47:58.180 align:middle line:84%
is, again, a Banach
space, since it's

00:47:58.180 --> 00:48:08.830 align:middle line:84%
a closed subspace of
the Banach space B1

00:48:08.830 --> 00:48:15.330 align:middle line:84%
cross B2 with this norm that I
defined in the earlier theorem.

00:48:15.330 --> 00:48:20.170 align:middle line:84%
Now, pi 1 and pi 2--
these are both continuous,

00:48:20.170 --> 00:48:23.380 align:middle line:84%
viewed now as maps
from the Banach space

00:48:23.380 --> 00:48:26.710 align:middle line:90%
of the graph to B1 and B2.

00:48:26.710 --> 00:48:32.770 align:middle line:90%


00:48:32.770 --> 00:48:42.250 align:middle line:84%
Pi 1 is a bounded linear
operator from the graph T, B1.

00:48:42.250 --> 00:48:52.230 align:middle line:84%
And pi 2 is a bounded
linear operator to B2.

00:48:52.230 --> 00:48:53.250 align:middle line:90%
Why is this?

00:48:53.250 --> 00:48:57.890 align:middle line:90%
I mean, this is pretty clear.

00:48:57.890 --> 00:49:03.860 align:middle line:84%
So the graph of the norm of
things in here are just--

00:49:03.860 --> 00:49:10.190 align:middle line:84%
so this is because if I take the
norm of pi of-- let me write it

00:49:10.190 --> 00:49:14.600 align:middle line:84%
u, v now, where v is
standing for T u--

00:49:14.600 --> 00:49:22.710 align:middle line:84%
this is equal to v, which
is less than or equal to--

00:49:22.710 --> 00:49:26.840 align:middle line:90%
so this is pi 2 of this one--

00:49:26.840 --> 00:49:37.300 align:middle line:84%
which is less than or equal
to, and then the same thing

00:49:37.300 --> 00:49:38.140 align:middle line:90%
with pi 1.

00:49:38.140 --> 00:49:44.270 align:middle line:90%


00:49:44.270 --> 00:49:50.120 align:middle line:84%
So pi 1 and pi 2 going from
the graph to B1 and B2--

00:49:50.120 --> 00:49:53.310 align:middle line:84%
these are bounded
linear operators.

00:49:53.310 --> 00:49:59.940 align:middle line:84%
And pi 1, when restricted to the
graph is, in fact, bijective.

00:49:59.940 --> 00:50:03.780 align:middle line:90%
It's one to one and onto.

00:50:03.780 --> 00:50:10.043 align:middle line:90%
So I've used "moreover" again.

00:50:10.043 --> 00:50:11.710 align:middle line:84%
So let me just write
moreover more over.

00:50:11.710 --> 00:50:15.310 align:middle line:90%


00:50:15.310 --> 00:50:33.690 align:middle line:84%
Pi one going from the graph
to B1 is one to one and onto,

00:50:33.690 --> 00:50:34.620 align:middle line:90%
bijective.

00:50:34.620 --> 00:50:37.510 align:middle line:90%


00:50:37.510 --> 00:50:42.820 align:middle line:84%
Everything in B1 gets mapped
to by pi 1 from the graph.

00:50:42.820 --> 00:50:47.680 align:middle line:84%
If you have something u1 here,
then its image is u1 T of u1.

00:50:47.680 --> 00:50:52.750 align:middle line:84%
That's a unique element in the
graph, since T is a function.

00:50:52.750 --> 00:50:56.350 align:middle line:84%
There's only one element
in the graph corresponding

00:50:56.350 --> 00:50:58.000 align:middle line:90%
to a given u.

00:50:58.000 --> 00:51:03.090 align:middle line:90%


00:51:03.090 --> 00:51:08.520 align:middle line:84%
And so let's pause
the proof here

00:51:08.520 --> 00:51:10.500 align:middle line:84%
because I forgot to
write a corollary

00:51:10.500 --> 00:51:13.170 align:middle line:90%
after the open mapping theorem.

00:51:13.170 --> 00:51:14.985 align:middle line:84%
In fact, let's
state it over here.

00:51:14.985 --> 00:51:18.450 align:middle line:90%


00:51:18.450 --> 00:51:23.570 align:middle line:84%
So this was the end of the proof
of the open mapping theorem.

00:51:23.570 --> 00:51:26.300 align:middle line:84%
So there's space
for the corollary,

00:51:26.300 --> 00:51:30.040 align:middle line:90%
which I wanted to write here.

00:51:30.040 --> 00:51:44.110 align:middle line:84%
It's the following-- if
B1 B2 are Banach spaces,

00:51:44.110 --> 00:51:47.890 align:middle line:84%
T is a bounded linear
operator from B1

00:51:47.890 --> 00:51:55.740 align:middle line:84%
to B2, which is bijective,
meaning one to one and onto,

00:51:55.740 --> 00:52:03.780 align:middle line:84%
so it has an inverse,
then T inverse

00:52:03.780 --> 00:52:11.591 align:middle line:84%
is a bounded linear
operator from B2 to B1.

00:52:11.591 --> 00:52:16.360 align:middle line:90%


00:52:16.360 --> 00:52:19.540 align:middle line:84%
So if I have a bounded linear
operator from one Banach

00:52:19.540 --> 00:52:21.880 align:middle line:84%
space to another
and it's bijective,

00:52:21.880 --> 00:52:24.690 align:middle line:84%
its inverse is
automatically continuous.

00:52:24.690 --> 00:52:29.230 align:middle line:90%


00:52:29.230 --> 00:52:31.870 align:middle line:84%
And the proof of that just
follows from the open mapping

00:52:31.870 --> 00:52:32.920 align:middle line:90%
theorem.

00:52:32.920 --> 00:52:36.700 align:middle line:84%
So I'm going to
write it in one line

00:52:36.700 --> 00:52:39.580 align:middle line:84%
because that's all the space
I'm going to give myself.

00:52:39.580 --> 00:52:46.330 align:middle line:84%
T inverse is continuous
if and only if,

00:52:46.330 --> 00:52:56.010 align:middle line:84%
for all u that's open,
for all open sets

00:52:56.010 --> 00:53:02.610 align:middle line:84%
u and v1 which is in the
image, the inverse image of T

00:53:02.610 --> 00:53:04.110 align:middle line:90%
inverse--

00:53:04.110 --> 00:53:06.240 align:middle line:84%
or I should say the
inverse image of u

00:53:06.240 --> 00:53:12.300 align:middle line:84%
by T inverse, which you can
just check is equal to T of u--

00:53:12.300 --> 00:53:14.430 align:middle line:90%
is open.

00:53:14.430 --> 00:53:17.880 align:middle line:84%
And that's true by the
open mapping theorem

00:53:17.880 --> 00:53:20.880 align:middle line:84%
since a bijective
map is surjective.

00:53:20.880 --> 00:53:27.990 align:middle line:84%
So every bijective bounded
linear operator automatically

00:53:27.990 --> 00:53:32.430 align:middle line:90%
has a bounded inverse.

00:53:32.430 --> 00:53:33.420 align:middle line:90%
It's also linear.

00:53:33.420 --> 00:53:37.320 align:middle line:84%
I mean, I didn't
say that, but if I

00:53:37.320 --> 00:53:39.870 align:middle line:84%
have a linear operator
which is bijective,

00:53:39.870 --> 00:53:43.150 align:middle line:90%
then its inverse is also linear.

00:53:43.150 --> 00:53:48.840 align:middle line:84%
So now coming back to this proof
of the closed graph theorem.

00:53:48.840 --> 00:53:54.960 align:middle line:84%
So we have the graph, which is
a Banach space in its own right

00:53:54.960 --> 00:54:00.224 align:middle line:84%
as a subset of B1 cross B2, as
a closed subset of B1 cross B2.

00:54:00.224 --> 00:54:06.600 align:middle line:84%
I have pi 1 and pi 2, which
are bounded linear operators

00:54:06.600 --> 00:54:07.770 align:middle line:90%
between B1 and B2.

00:54:07.770 --> 00:54:09.930 align:middle line:84%
I didn't say that
they're linear,

00:54:09.930 --> 00:54:12.690 align:middle line:90%
but that should be clear.

00:54:12.690 --> 00:54:19.480 align:middle line:90%


00:54:19.480 --> 00:54:27.870 align:middle line:84%
And pi 1, when restricted to the
graph, is one to one and onto.

00:54:27.870 --> 00:54:32.100 align:middle line:84%
Pi 1 here just takes the first
element of what's in the graph

00:54:32.100 --> 00:54:33.060 align:middle line:90%
and spits out--

00:54:33.060 --> 00:54:38.130 align:middle line:84%
let me, instead of having T u
there, let me have v like that.

00:54:38.130 --> 00:54:41.510 align:middle line:90%


00:54:41.510 --> 00:54:43.490 align:middle line:84%
We just know that
v is equal to T u.

00:54:43.490 --> 00:54:46.850 align:middle line:90%


00:54:46.850 --> 00:54:50.000 align:middle line:84%
And so this is one
to one and onto.

00:54:50.000 --> 00:54:51.110 align:middle line:90%
It's bijective.

00:54:51.110 --> 00:54:54.860 align:middle line:90%


00:54:54.860 --> 00:54:59.270 align:middle line:84%
Thus, it has an inverse which
is a bounded linear operator,

00:54:59.270 --> 00:55:01.310 align:middle line:84%
by the corollary that
I stated over there.

00:55:01.310 --> 00:55:13.610 align:middle line:90%


00:55:13.610 --> 00:55:25.450 align:middle line:84%
It is a bounded linear
operator, which implies

00:55:25.450 --> 00:55:32.330 align:middle line:90%
that T, which I can write as--

00:55:32.330 --> 00:55:35.780 align:middle line:84%
I shouldn't have S inverse,
defined to be the inverse.

00:55:35.780 --> 00:55:40.370 align:middle line:84%
And therefore, T, which
is equal to S of pi 1

00:55:40.370 --> 00:55:47.420 align:middle line:84%
going from B1 to B2
is now the product

00:55:47.420 --> 00:55:52.640 align:middle line:84%
of two bounded linear operators,
pi 1 restricted from B1

00:55:52.640 --> 00:55:54.540 align:middle line:90%
from B1 up to the graph.

00:55:54.540 --> 00:55:59.570 align:middle line:90%
And then S-- no, no, no.

00:55:59.570 --> 00:56:00.860 align:middle line:90%
I'm messing this all up.

00:56:00.860 --> 00:56:10.300 align:middle line:90%
Hold on-- S pi 2.

00:56:10.300 --> 00:56:18.130 align:middle line:90%


00:56:18.130 --> 00:56:19.760 align:middle line:90%
OK, now this makes sense.

00:56:19.760 --> 00:56:23.830 align:middle line:84%
So S, which is the
inverse of pi 1,

00:56:23.830 --> 00:56:26.260 align:middle line:90%
is a bounded linear operator.

00:56:26.260 --> 00:56:28.180 align:middle line:84%
Pi 2 is a bounded
linear operator.

00:56:28.180 --> 00:56:29.890 align:middle line:84%
And therefore, their
composition is also

00:56:29.890 --> 00:56:33.790 align:middle line:84%
a bounded linear operator,
which implies that T

00:56:33.790 --> 00:56:35.440 align:middle line:90%
is a bounded linear operator.

00:56:35.440 --> 00:56:44.848 align:middle line:90%


00:56:44.848 --> 00:56:47.390 align:middle line:84%
So I made kind of a mess of that
having to go back and forth,

00:56:47.390 --> 00:56:50.075 align:middle line:90%
but the proof is simple enough.

00:56:50.075 --> 00:56:57.400 align:middle line:90%


00:56:57.400 --> 00:57:04.860 align:middle line:84%
I'm sure Professor Melrose would
have just drawn the picture,

00:57:04.860 --> 00:57:06.750 align:middle line:84%
but I decided to
make a mess of it.

00:57:06.750 --> 00:57:10.970 align:middle line:90%


00:57:10.970 --> 00:57:17.480 align:middle line:84%
So those are some pretty
important theorems

00:57:17.480 --> 00:57:19.580 align:middle line:84%
that follow from the
Baire category theorem.

00:57:19.580 --> 00:57:22.610 align:middle line:84%
So we got uniform boundedness
from Baire category.

00:57:22.610 --> 00:57:24.530 align:middle line:84%
We got open mapping
from Baire category.

00:57:24.530 --> 00:57:29.150 align:middle line:84%
We got closed graph
from open mapping.

00:57:29.150 --> 00:57:36.790 align:middle line:84%
If you're a lover of logic,
think about it a little bit--

00:57:36.790 --> 00:57:39.730 align:middle line:84%
open mapping implies
closed graph,

00:57:39.730 --> 00:57:45.130 align:middle line:84%
but you can also show
that closed graph implies

00:57:45.130 --> 00:57:45.790 align:middle line:90%
open mapping.

00:57:45.790 --> 00:57:48.435 align:middle line:84%
So as logical statements,
they're equivalent.

00:57:48.435 --> 00:57:51.160 align:middle line:90%


00:57:51.160 --> 00:58:05.490 align:middle line:84%
Now, we're going to move on
to the Hahn Banach theorem.

00:58:05.490 --> 00:58:07.170 align:middle line:84%
So I haven't done
many examples here

00:58:07.170 --> 00:58:11.410 align:middle line:84%
going into this kind of general
theory, but don't worry.

00:58:11.410 --> 00:58:16.110 align:middle line:84%
There will be plenty of examples
in the assignments of using

00:58:16.110 --> 00:58:18.110 align:middle line:90%
these theorems, and so on.

00:58:18.110 --> 00:58:19.585 align:middle line:90%
So the Hahn Banach theorem--

00:58:19.585 --> 00:58:25.620 align:middle line:90%


00:58:25.620 --> 00:58:29.950 align:middle line:84%
these theorems before were all
kind of answering a question.

00:58:29.950 --> 00:58:34.515 align:middle line:84%
Maybe I didn't state the
questions as clearly.

00:58:34.515 --> 00:58:37.230 align:middle line:90%


00:58:37.230 --> 00:58:39.750 align:middle line:90%
Closed graph is kind of--

00:58:39.750 --> 00:58:41.790 align:middle line:84%
well, it doesn't so
much answer question

00:58:41.790 --> 00:58:45.330 align:middle line:84%
as give us an alternative
to proving continuity.

00:58:45.330 --> 00:58:48.240 align:middle line:84%
Open mapping you can
think of as trying

00:58:48.240 --> 00:58:49.830 align:middle line:84%
to answer this
question-- if I have

00:58:49.830 --> 00:58:52.140 align:middle line:84%
a bijective bounded
linear operator,

00:58:52.140 --> 00:58:56.790 align:middle line:84%
is its inverse a
bounded linear operator?

00:58:56.790 --> 00:59:00.270 align:middle line:84%
And uniform boundedness
is the answer

00:59:00.270 --> 00:59:02.640 align:middle line:84%
to the question for
at least a sequence

00:59:02.640 --> 00:59:05.280 align:middle line:84%
of bounded linear operators,
does pointwise convergence

00:59:05.280 --> 00:59:09.150 align:middle line:84%
imply, or pointwise boundedness
imply uniform boundedness?

00:59:09.150 --> 00:59:15.000 align:middle line:90%


00:59:15.000 --> 00:59:18.930 align:middle line:84%
Now, the question that the Hahn
Banach theorem tries to answer

00:59:18.930 --> 00:59:20.310 align:middle line:90%
is the following--

00:59:20.310 --> 00:59:23.900 align:middle line:90%


00:59:23.900 --> 00:59:49.410 align:middle line:84%
given the general
non-trivial normed norm space

00:59:49.410 --> 01:00:05.300 align:middle line:84%
V, is the dual space given
by simply the zero vector?

01:00:05.300 --> 01:00:09.320 align:middle line:84%
So at the end of last class,
I defined the dual space.

01:00:09.320 --> 01:00:14.930 align:middle line:84%
Recall, this is equal to
the bounded linear operators

01:00:14.930 --> 01:00:20.810 align:middle line:84%
from the norm space V to
the field of scalars which

01:00:20.810 --> 01:00:24.740 align:middle line:84%
is a Banach space
because it's a space

01:00:24.740 --> 01:00:30.230 align:middle line:84%
of bounded linear operators from
a norm space to a Banach space,

01:00:30.230 --> 01:00:32.890 align:middle line:90%
so it's a Banach space.

01:00:32.890 --> 01:00:37.645 align:middle line:84%
And we usually refer to
elements of the dual as--

01:00:37.645 --> 01:00:40.610 align:middle line:90%


01:00:40.610 --> 01:00:42.290 align:middle line:84%
I'm not sure if I
said this last time,

01:00:42.290 --> 01:00:45.590 align:middle line:84%
but we don't refer to them
as bounded linear operators

01:00:45.590 --> 01:00:48.860 align:middle line:84%
from the vector space
to the field of scalars,

01:00:48.860 --> 01:00:51.380 align:middle line:90%
but as functionals.

01:00:51.380 --> 01:00:54.710 align:middle line:84%
Because the classical space
is where function spaces--

01:00:54.710 --> 01:00:59.700 align:middle line:84%
the classical Banach spaces
were spaces of function.

01:00:59.700 --> 01:01:03.260 align:middle line:84%
So the things that ate
them and spat out a number

01:01:03.260 --> 01:01:08.750 align:middle line:84%
were called
functionals, so evolving

01:01:08.750 --> 01:01:12.500 align:middle line:84%
from function of functions
and functions of lines.

01:01:12.500 --> 01:01:16.470 align:middle line:90%


01:01:16.470 --> 01:01:19.710 align:middle line:84%
So the question is, if I
have just the norm space,

01:01:19.710 --> 01:01:24.510 align:middle line:84%
is the dual space kind
of nontrivial in general?

01:01:24.510 --> 01:01:28.590 align:middle line:84%
So last time, I hinted
that for certain spaces,

01:01:28.590 --> 01:01:33.690 align:middle line:84%
you can actually write down
the dual space explicitly.

01:01:33.690 --> 01:01:37.935 align:middle line:84%
You can at least identify the
dual space in an explicit way.

01:01:37.935 --> 01:01:45.100 align:middle line:90%


01:01:45.100 --> 01:01:49.060 align:middle line:84%
I hinted last time that
the dual of little l

01:01:49.060 --> 01:01:55.150 align:middle line:84%
p prime is, in fact,
equal to l p prime,

01:01:55.150 --> 01:02:03.740 align:middle line:84%
where p prime is defined
as the dual exponent.

01:02:03.740 --> 01:02:06.620 align:middle line:84%
So 1 over p plus 1
over p prime equals 1.

01:02:06.620 --> 01:02:11.210 align:middle line:84%
And this is for p bigger
than or equal to 1 and less

01:02:11.210 --> 01:02:16.580 align:middle line:84%
than infinity, but not
for p equals infinity.

01:02:16.580 --> 01:02:18.820 align:middle line:84%
And if you remember
last time, C0,

01:02:18.820 --> 01:02:24.295 align:middle line:84%
which is a set of sequences
which converts to 0--

01:02:24.295 --> 01:02:26.920 align:middle line:84%
I can't remember if I wrote this
down at the end of last class,

01:02:26.920 --> 01:02:32.860 align:middle line:84%
but you can also identify its
dual space with little l1.

01:02:32.860 --> 01:02:39.140 align:middle line:90%


01:02:39.140 --> 01:02:42.020 align:middle line:84%
So just to give you some
examples of spaces that do

01:02:42.020 --> 01:02:47.330 align:middle line:90%
have nontrivial dual space--

01:02:47.330 --> 01:02:50.870 align:middle line:84%
examples of norm spaces which
have nontrivial dual space

01:02:50.870 --> 01:02:52.595 align:middle line:84%
are given by the
little l p spaces.

01:02:52.595 --> 01:02:56.500 align:middle line:90%


01:02:56.500 --> 01:02:58.420 align:middle line:84%
But now the question
is, in general

01:02:58.420 --> 01:03:01.740 align:middle line:84%
for a norm space, is the
space of functionals,

01:03:01.740 --> 01:03:04.270 align:middle line:90%
is the dual space nontrivial?

01:03:04.270 --> 01:03:07.570 align:middle line:84%
And this is a statement
of the Hahn Banach theorem

01:03:07.570 --> 01:03:12.850 align:middle line:84%
that there's, in fact, a lot
of elements in the dual space.

01:03:12.850 --> 01:03:17.260 align:middle line:84%
Now, we're not going to get to
the statement or proof of that

01:03:17.260 --> 01:03:18.010 align:middle line:90%
in this lecture.

01:03:18.010 --> 01:03:26.270 align:middle line:90%


01:03:26.270 --> 01:03:33.420 align:middle line:84%
Because we first need to
at least go over, or state,

01:03:33.420 --> 01:03:38.188 align:middle line:84%
or you can say result,
axiom from set theory

01:03:38.188 --> 01:03:38.855 align:middle line:90%
that we'll need.

01:03:38.855 --> 01:03:48.710 align:middle line:90%


01:03:48.710 --> 01:03:54.710 align:middle line:84%
So first, like I
said, we need an axiom

01:03:54.710 --> 01:03:58.110 align:middle line:84%
or recall an axiom, a certain
lemma, from set theory.

01:03:58.110 --> 01:04:06.100 align:middle line:84%
So first, let me set down
the appropriate terminology.

01:04:06.100 --> 01:04:22.900 align:middle line:84%
So partial order on a
set E is a relation,

01:04:22.900 --> 01:04:27.910 align:middle line:84%
meaning just a subset of E
cross E, denoted by this--

01:04:27.910 --> 01:04:31.300 align:middle line:84%
but we usually don't identify
it as a subset of E cross E--

01:04:31.300 --> 01:04:34.820 align:middle line:90%


01:04:34.820 --> 01:04:40.005 align:middle line:90%
such that three things occur.

01:04:40.005 --> 01:04:42.255 align:middle line:84%
So you think of it kind of
as a less than or equal to.

01:04:42.255 --> 01:04:47.790 align:middle line:90%


01:04:47.790 --> 01:04:54.770 align:middle line:84%
For all E in E, E is related
to E. I will say less than

01:04:54.770 --> 01:04:56.780 align:middle line:84%
or equal to, even though
this may have nothing

01:04:56.780 --> 01:05:00.690 align:middle line:84%
to do with less
than or equal to.

01:05:00.690 --> 01:05:11.360 align:middle line:84%
For all e and f in capital E,
E less than or equal to f and f

01:05:11.360 --> 01:05:14.750 align:middle line:84%
less than or equal to
e, these two assumptions

01:05:14.750 --> 01:05:17.810 align:middle line:84%
imply that e is the
same element as f.

01:05:17.810 --> 01:05:20.350 align:middle line:90%


01:05:20.350 --> 01:05:26.060 align:middle line:84%
And transitivity-- so
this is reflexivity.

01:05:26.060 --> 01:05:28.920 align:middle line:84%
I'm not sure what would
you call this one.

01:05:28.920 --> 01:05:31.230 align:middle line:90%
I can't exactly remember.

01:05:31.230 --> 01:05:37.800 align:middle line:84%
For all e, f, g in E, the
two assumptions E less than

01:05:37.800 --> 01:05:45.630 align:middle line:84%
or equal to f and f less than or
equal to g implies e less than

01:05:45.630 --> 01:05:47.530 align:middle line:90%
or equal to g.

01:05:47.530 --> 01:05:51.585 align:middle line:84%
So this is the definition
of a partial order.

01:05:51.585 --> 01:05:57.070 align:middle line:90%


01:05:57.070 --> 01:06:13.690 align:middle line:84%
So to go with this, we say
an upper bound of a set

01:06:13.690 --> 01:06:27.680 align:middle line:84%
D contained in E is
an element e in E such

01:06:27.680 --> 01:06:35.040 align:middle line:84%
that for all d in capital D,
d is less than or equal to e.

01:06:35.040 --> 01:06:48.720 align:middle line:84%
And a maximal
element of the set E

01:06:48.720 --> 01:06:59.500 align:middle line:84%
is an element e in E,
which nothing lies bigger

01:06:59.500 --> 01:07:00.790 align:middle line:90%
than it, essentially.

01:07:00.790 --> 01:07:05.240 align:middle line:84%
Nothing maximizes
it or majorizes it,

01:07:05.240 --> 01:07:19.860 align:middle line:84%
such that if f is in E and e is
less than or equal to f, then e

01:07:19.860 --> 01:07:21.940 align:middle line:90%
is equal to f.

01:07:21.940 --> 01:07:33.240 align:middle line:84%
And a similar definition
for a minimal element.

01:07:33.240 --> 01:07:35.270 align:middle line:84%
So this was the definition
of a maximal element.

01:07:35.270 --> 01:07:39.140 align:middle line:90%


01:07:39.140 --> 01:07:44.450 align:middle line:84%
And this is the definition
for a minimal element.

01:07:44.450 --> 01:07:47.270 align:middle line:84%
Now, note the maximum
element may not

01:07:47.270 --> 01:07:50.190 align:middle line:84%
sit above everything
in E necessarily.

01:07:50.190 --> 01:07:54.770 align:middle line:84%
It may just be kind of off to
the side of everything in E.

01:07:54.770 --> 01:07:59.650 align:middle line:84%
Because this doesn't
assert that you can always

01:07:59.650 --> 01:08:03.850 align:middle line:84%
check to see if
between two elements,

01:08:03.850 --> 01:08:05.260 align:middle line:90%
if one is bigger than the other.

01:08:05.260 --> 01:08:09.010 align:middle line:84%
That's something a
little more restrictive,

01:08:09.010 --> 01:08:10.450 align:middle line:90%
which is the following--

01:08:10.450 --> 01:08:14.040 align:middle line:90%


01:08:14.040 --> 01:08:24.109 align:middle line:84%
if E and less than or equal
to is a partially ordered set,

01:08:24.109 --> 01:08:41.840 align:middle line:84%
meaning a set with a partial
order, a chain in E--

01:08:41.840 --> 01:08:46.189 align:middle line:90%


01:08:46.189 --> 01:08:50.600 align:middle line:84%
so maybe a better way to say
this is a set C is a chain if,

01:08:50.600 --> 01:08:55.510 align:middle line:84%
but a chain in E is a set
C such that for all e,

01:08:55.510 --> 01:09:06.910 align:middle line:84%
f in C, either e is less
than or equal to f or f

01:09:06.910 --> 01:09:10.660 align:middle line:90%
is less than or equal to e.

01:09:10.660 --> 01:09:13.210 align:middle line:84%
So a chain is
something so that you

01:09:13.210 --> 01:09:15.580 align:middle line:84%
can, for any two
elements in the set,

01:09:15.580 --> 01:09:18.520 align:middle line:84%
compare whether one is
bigger than the other.

01:09:18.520 --> 01:09:21.040 align:middle line:90%
That's what a chain is.

01:09:21.040 --> 01:09:25.420 align:middle line:84%
But for a general partial
order, it doesn't necessarily

01:09:25.420 --> 01:09:27.154 align:middle line:84%
need to be the case
that you can always

01:09:27.154 --> 01:09:29.029 align:middle line:84%
check to see if one is
bigger than the other.

01:09:29.029 --> 01:09:31.210 align:middle line:84%
For example, your
partial order could be

01:09:31.210 --> 01:09:33.609 align:middle line:90%
on the power set of some set.

01:09:33.609 --> 01:09:36.319 align:middle line:84%
And the partial
order is inclusion,

01:09:36.319 --> 01:09:40.899 align:middle line:84%
whether one subset is
a contained in another.

01:09:40.899 --> 01:09:42.760 align:middle line:84%
Then it satisfies
these three properties,

01:09:42.760 --> 01:09:46.180 align:middle line:84%
but there's sets that cannot
be compared to each other.

01:09:46.180 --> 01:09:52.790 align:middle line:90%


01:09:52.790 --> 01:10:00.265 align:middle line:84%
So let me write this
as lemma due to Zorn.

01:10:00.265 --> 01:10:01.515 align:middle line:90%
We're not going to prove this.

01:10:01.515 --> 01:10:05.080 align:middle line:90%


01:10:05.080 --> 01:10:10.110 align:middle line:84%
Even though I'm writing "lemma,"
just take it as an axiom,

01:10:10.110 --> 01:10:17.490 align:middle line:84%
an axiom of Fraenkel set
theory that goes with it,

01:10:17.490 --> 01:10:29.310 align:middle line:84%
which is the following-- that
if every chain in a nonempty--

01:10:29.310 --> 01:10:32.190 align:middle line:84%
of course, we're considering
nontrivial stuff, so

01:10:32.190 --> 01:10:33.360 align:middle line:90%
in a nonempty--

01:10:33.360 --> 01:10:39.070 align:middle line:90%


01:10:39.070 --> 01:10:51.250 align:middle line:84%
partially ordered set,
E has an upper bound,

01:10:51.250 --> 01:11:02.515 align:middle line:90%
then E has a maximal element.

01:11:02.515 --> 01:11:05.550 align:middle line:90%


01:11:05.550 --> 01:11:09.720 align:middle line:84%
So if you can check that every
chain has an upper bound,

01:11:09.720 --> 01:11:13.950 align:middle line:84%
then you get to conclude
that the partially ordered

01:11:13.950 --> 01:11:17.380 align:middle line:90%
set has a maximal element.

01:11:17.380 --> 01:11:23.590 align:middle line:84%
Now, we'll give a
simple application

01:11:23.590 --> 01:11:28.850 align:middle line:84%
of this at the start
of the next lecture.

01:11:28.850 --> 01:11:36.550 align:middle line:84%
But first, let me put this into
your brains to marinate on.

01:11:36.550 --> 01:11:40.270 align:middle line:84%
So we're going to use Zorn's
Lemma to prove the Hahn Banach

01:11:40.270 --> 01:11:40.770 align:middle line:90%
theorem.

01:11:40.770 --> 01:11:46.810 align:middle line:84%
And I'll go into why
that is next time.

01:11:46.810 --> 01:11:49.240 align:middle line:84%
But you can use Zorn's
Lemma to prove other things,

01:11:49.240 --> 01:11:51.470 align:middle line:84%
and it is used to
prove other things.

01:11:51.470 --> 01:11:55.390 align:middle line:84%
And first off, from
Zorn's Lemma you

01:11:55.390 --> 01:12:00.743 align:middle line:84%
can, in fact, prove
the axiom of choice,

01:12:00.743 --> 01:12:02.410 align:middle line:84%
which says given any
collection of sets,

01:12:02.410 --> 01:12:11.730 align:middle line:84%
you can essentially choose an
element from each set stated

01:12:11.730 --> 01:12:13.470 align:middle line:90%
in a very precise way.

01:12:13.470 --> 01:12:15.240 align:middle line:84%
Another way, which
we're going to use

01:12:15.240 --> 01:12:18.300 align:middle line:84%
at the beginning of next time,
is to prove the following--

01:12:18.300 --> 01:12:21.540 align:middle line:84%
but first let me
make a definition.

01:12:21.540 --> 01:12:26.770 align:middle line:90%


01:12:26.770 --> 01:12:32.780 align:middle line:84%
A Hamel basis, H
which is a subset--

01:12:32.780 --> 01:12:34.670 align:middle line:90%
not a subspace, but a subset--

01:12:34.670 --> 01:12:55.610 align:middle line:84%
of V, a vector space, is
a linearly independent set

01:12:55.610 --> 01:13:20.560 align:middle line:84%
such that every element of v
is a finite linear combination

01:13:20.560 --> 01:13:24.070 align:middle line:90%
of elements of H.

01:13:24.070 --> 01:13:30.540 align:middle line:84%
So from linear algebra,
for finite dimensions,

01:13:30.540 --> 01:13:35.850 align:middle line:84%
this is in some sense how one
can define the dimension is you

01:13:35.850 --> 01:13:40.130 align:middle line:84%
find a basis and then the
cardinality of that basis

01:13:40.130 --> 01:13:42.620 align:middle line:90%
is always the same.

01:13:42.620 --> 01:13:48.410 align:middle line:84%
So a Hamel basis for R
n is just the vectors

01:13:48.410 --> 01:13:53.190 align:middle line:84%
with 1 in one of the
entries and 0 otherwise.

01:13:53.190 --> 01:13:57.710 align:middle line:84%
So for R 2, it's
just 1, 0, and 0, 1.

01:13:57.710 --> 01:14:02.910 align:middle line:84%
A Hamel basis for, let's
say, little l1 would

01:14:02.910 --> 01:14:07.560 align:middle line:84%
be one in the first,
followed by 0's, 1

01:14:07.560 --> 01:14:11.640 align:middle line:84%
in the second, 0 elsewhere,
1 in the third, 0 elsewhere--

01:14:11.640 --> 01:14:12.750 align:middle line:90%
the set of these elements.

01:14:12.750 --> 01:14:16.060 align:middle line:90%


01:14:16.060 --> 01:14:18.970 align:middle line:84%
Now, the question is,
does every vector space

01:14:18.970 --> 01:14:21.370 align:middle line:90%
have a Hamel basis?

01:14:21.370 --> 01:14:24.220 align:middle line:84%
And using, next
time, Zorn's Lemma,

01:14:24.220 --> 01:14:29.110 align:middle line:84%
we'll show that indeed, every
vector space has a Hamel basis.

01:14:29.110 --> 01:14:37.050 align:middle line:84%
And in fact, that Hamel
basis can be quite big.

01:14:37.050 --> 01:14:42.450 align:middle line:84%
But that'll be a simple
application we do next time,

01:14:42.450 --> 01:14:46.530 align:middle line:84%
is that via Zorn, you can
show that every vector

01:14:46.530 --> 01:14:48.870 align:middle line:90%
space has a Hamel basis.

01:14:48.870 --> 01:14:50.870 align:middle line:90%
We'll stop there.

01:14:50.870 --> 01:14:57.000 align:middle line:90%